    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Pontus grew out of the turmoil that followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-i-of-pontus">Mithridates I Ktistes</a> (‘the Founder’), belonged to a noble family of Persian origin that had ruled the Greek city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cius">Kios</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>. Around 302 BCE, fearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus I Monophthalmus</a>, who had put a kinsman to death, he fled east with a few followers to the stronghold of Kimiata in Paphlagonia and built up a following among the peoples of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. He took the royal title in 281 BCE, in the confusion after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-corupedium">Battle of Corupedium</a>, and his kingdom, centred on the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesilirmak">Iris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycus-phrygia">Lycus</a> rivers, took its name from the Greek word for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, Pontos. The early capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amaseia</a>, a fortress city in a deep gorge of the Iris, where the kings’ tombs were carved into the cliffs below the citadel. His successors Ariobarzanes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridate-play">Mithridates</a> II and Mithridates III extended their control towards the coast and married into the Seleucid dynasty. Pharnaces I captured the great Greek port of Sinope in 183 BCE and made it the royal capital, turning Pontus into a Black Sea power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trabzon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trabzon.jpg" alt="Trabzon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trebisonda,_ex-chiesa_della_panagia_Chrysokephalos,_oggi_moschea_fatih,_esterno_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francesco Bini, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pontus combined very different worlds. Along the coast lay old Greek colonies such as Sinope, Amisos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trapezus</a>, centres of trade, fishing and shipbuilding, while the interior was a land of villages, fortresses and great temple estates. The temple of the goddess Ma at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comana-pontica">Comana Pontica</a>, served according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> by more than 6,000 temple slaves and ruled by a high priest second in rank only to the king, was among the richest sanctuaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and the moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/men-deity">Men Pharnakou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus-stratios">Zeus Stratios</a>, probably a Greek form of an Iranian deity, were also widely honoured. The ruling class mixed Persian aristocratic traditions, including Iranian names and cults, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and culture, and royal coins and inscriptions were in Greek. The land was rich in resources: grain and fruit from the river valleys, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from the forested mountains, iron from the territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalybes">Chalybes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and alum, and tunny fish from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. These resources, together with the grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> of the Black Sea, paid for armies, fleets and an ambitious court. Strabo, who was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amaseia</a> around 64 BCE into a family that had served the kings, gives a detailed description of the country in his <em>Geography</em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war.jpg" alt="Third Mithridatic War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1stMithritadicwar89BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Yakubovich, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under its early kings Pontus was a second-rank power, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI Eupator</a> made it the most dangerous enemy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> faced in the east. His general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diophantus-general">Diophantus</a> campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> around 110–107 BCE, bringing the Greek city of Chersonesus and the Bosporan Kingdom under Pontic rule, and Colchis and Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> were also annexed. The royal army combined a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> on the Macedonian model, cavalry, scythed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and large contingents of allies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, Scythia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatia">Sarmatia</a> and Galatia, while the Pontic navy dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. Ancient writers credit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridate-play">Mithridates</a> with enormous forces at the start of the First Mithridatic War, such as Appian’s 250,000 infantry, 40,000 cavalry and 300 decked warships, figures that are probably inflated. In 88 BCE he overran the Roman province of Asia and sent armies into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, but failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> defeated his generals at Chaeronea and Orchomenus in 86–85 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucullus</a> broke his siege of Cyzicus in 73–72 BCE and defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cabira">Cabira</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> crushed his army on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycus-phrygia">Lycus</a> river in 66 BCE, near the site where he then founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicopolis-lesser-armenia">Nicopolis</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-v-of-pontus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithridates-v-of-pontus.jpg" alt="Mithridates V of Pontus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-v-of-pontus">Mithridates V of Pontus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monete_ellenistiche,_regno_del_ponto,_mitridate_V_euergetes,_tetradracma_in_argento.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francesco Bini, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pontus was a hereditary monarchy in which the king stood at the centre of power, surrounded by a court of ‘friends’, relatives and officials in the Hellenistic manner, with Persian elements in its titles and rituals. The dynasty claimed descent from the Achaemenid royal house, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a> also boasted descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, presenting himself as heir to both Persian and Macedonian kingship. The interior was governed through royal fortresses and districts under appointed officials, while the Greek cities kept their own councils and magistrates under royal supervision and the great temple states remained semi-autonomous under their priests. The kings used marriage and diplomacy to secure their position: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridate-play">Mithridates</a> II married a Seleucid princess, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-v-of-pontus">Mithridates V</a> sent help to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a> and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristonicus">Aristonicus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and was rewarded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, which Rome later withdrew, and Mithridates VI married his daughter Cleopatra to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Mithridates VI himself came to power after a regency of his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laodice-vi">Laodice</a>, whom he imprisoned around 113 BCE. Succession disputes and court intrigue remained frequent, and the kings relied heavily on Greek generals, admirals and advisers to run their wars and their finances.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mitridate-re-di-ponto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mitridate-re-di-ponto.jpg" alt="Mitridate, re di Ponto" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mitridate-re-di-ponto">Mitridate, re di Ponto</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Wolfgang_Amadeus_Mozart_at_the_age_of_13_in_Verona,_1770.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pontus mattered most as the base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a>, whose wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> lasted, with intervals, from 89 to 63 BCE and shaped the politics of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mithridatic-war">First Mithridatic War</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> the command and the army that he turned against Rome itself, and the Third made the careers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucullus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>; the extraordinary command granted to Pompey under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-manilia">Lex Manilia</a> in 66 BCE was a milestone in the concentration of power that ended the Republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridate-play">Mithridates</a>’ order in 88 BCE to kill the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Italians in Asia, the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiatic-vespers">Asiatic Vespers</a>, in which ancient sources claim that 80,000 or more people died, became notorious, and the welcome he received as a liberator in Greek cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> showed how resented Roman rule had become. Pompey’s settlement joined western Pontus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> to form the province of Bithynia et Pontus and gave the rest to client rulers. The king himself became a legend: his supposed immunity to poison, built up by taking small doses, gave the word mithridatism, and the antidote called mithridatium was prescribed in European medicine for centuries. He inspired works from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-racine">Racine</a>’s <em>Mithridate</em> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-amadeus-mozart">Mozart</a>’s opera <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitridate-re-di-ponto">Mitridate, re di Ponto</a></em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sulla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulla.jpg" alt="Sulla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Q._Pompeius_Rufus,_denarius,_54_BC,_RRC_434-1_(Sulla_only).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The end of the Kingdom of Pontus followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a>’s third war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>’s campaigns the king had to give up his conquests under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-dardanos">Peace of Dardanus</a> in 85 BCE, but he kept his kingdom and rebuilt his strength, survived a short <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-mithridatic-war">Second Mithridatic War</a> against the Roman governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-murena">Murena</a> in 83–81 BCE, and in 73 BCE went to war again after Rome inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucullus</a> defeated him, forced him to take refuge with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 71 BCE and occupied Pontus, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridate-play">Mithridates</a> returned in 68–67 BCE and destroyed a Roman army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zela">Zela</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, given the command in 66 BCE, routed him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycus-phrygia">Lycus</a>. Mithridates escaped through Colchis and round the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the Bosporan Kingdom, where he planned to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by land. His army and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-ii-of-pontus">Pharnaces II</a> turned against him, and in 63 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panticapaeum">Panticapaeum</a> he died after a failed attempt to poison himself, reportedly killed at his own request by a Gallic officer named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bituitus">Bituitus</a>. Pompey annexed western Pontus and distributed the rest among client rulers. Pharnaces II kept the Bosporus and later tried to recover his father’s kingdom, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> defeated him at Zela in 47 BCE, the victory he summed up as ‘veni, vidi, vici’.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Pontus fused Persian dynastic tradition with Greek city culture on the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s most persistent eastern enemy, whose three wars helped make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and reshaped the politics of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>.</em></p>

